-- The macros: every op the cards use that is not a primitive, written over
-- the ones that are.
--
-- `docs/arena-ruleset-spec.md` §2 is the decision — nineteen primitives, and
-- one `DEFINE OP` here for each of the thirty-one rows `OP_CLASS` marks
-- *macro*. A macro is not a deletion: `power(…)` keeps its `OP_SCHEMA` row,
-- keeps printing as itself and keeps round-tripping by that name; this file
-- says only what it *means*, and `rulesets/expand.ts` is what lowers a program
-- written in these ops to a program written in the primitives.
--
-- **Five rows are not declared yet, and the table below is why.** Writing one
-- that silently dropped an argument would be worse than none: it would
-- validate, it would expand, the sweep in `scripts/verify/language.ts` would
-- pass, and the rule it claimed to explain would be wrong. Each row names the
-- one thing it waits on. The expander is built and tested against fixtures, so
-- the first row that becomes writable is a declaration here and nothing else.
--
-- ── what each row waits on ────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
--   param     answered (#273): inside a DEFINE OP body `$name` may stand in
--             every field position — a duration, a side, an area, a mode or
--             any closed list (declared `word`), a list of strings
--             (`strings`), a whole program (`ops`) — and in a selector's
--             count, `TOP n`, side and area.
--   count     answered (#137): a selector's `TOP n` and its plain `n IN …`
--             count are one slot type, `count`, which a macro may fill with
--             a `number` or an `amount` — X and `handUpTo(4)` included —
--             because `draw`, `discard`, `damage` and `addLife` take `n` as
--             an amount. No selector *runs* counted by an expression: the
--             lowered move or choice is read back as the spelling it stands
--             for first (`moveAs`, `discardAs`, `engine/script-schema.ts`).
--   replace   answered (#125, #232, #436, and #137 for the two moments it
--             added): `replace` is an op, its `event` names `attack` and
--             `counter` beside the departures, and `replaceAs` reads an
--             attack or a counter replaced by nothing back as
--             `negateAttack`/`negateCounter`.
--   subject   answered (#275): `modifyAttr` reaches a *player*
--             (`energyMarkers`) and the *battle in progress* (`guard`) as
--             well as a card, and the card's own six new attributes are
--             declared in `attributes.rules` (spec §2.5-1, §2.5-3).
--   branch    a template's body is the same steps whichever arguments the
--             call gave, and three rows mean different *acts* depending on
--             which they gave. `resolvingPlay` with `instead` is a replaced
--             play — `replace(event: play, with: { moveTo(target:
--             [resolving], to: $instead, position: $position) })`, exactly
--             what its own case already hands `replaceResolvingPlay` — but
--             with `mode: rest` or `negated: true` the play *happens*, only
--             in another manner (9-6), and that body's required `to` would
--             refuse the call. `comboFrom` is a move to the Combo Area plus
--             a `negate` only when `negated` is true. `look` takes a `TOP` or
--             a `BOTTOM` by `from`, and a whole area with no count at all
--             when `area` is not the deck. Each needs a body that can depend
--             on an argument — a step kept only when a parameter is given or
--             true, or one body per value of a closed list — which is a
--             grammar change to `DEFINE OP` (§3b), not a declaration here.
--   audience  `reveal` shows a card to both players; `look` is the same act
--             with a narrower audience (20-11), and the field that says which
--             does not exist: `reveal` needs an `audience` (`you | both`,
--             default `both`) its case reads as "no log, no public reveal".
--   maths     an amount cannot subtract with a parameter on its right, so
--             "until you have n life" — `TOP life($side) - $n IN
--             $side.life` — cannot be written (spec §2.5-5): `plus`'s right
--             side is a printed number, and no hole can stand there.
--   bind      `mill`'s `as` names the milled cards for a later clause outside
--             the call ("if that card is red"). A string hole can carry the
--             name into a `choose` or a `reveal` (`as: $as`), but the move
--             after it would have to *read* that name as a ref, and a hole
--             in a ref position is replaced by the argument — the string,
--             not `{ var }` of it. The row waits on nothing this file's
--             grammar could answer, so it stays a native op.
--   cause     answered (#274) for `ko`; `comboFrom` would also need a
--             `moveAs` shape for its 5-7-2 guard (a battle on its master's
--             side) and its `youCombo`/`opponentCombos` moments, which a
--             plain `moveTo(cause: combo)` neither checks nor pends.
--
-- ── the thirty-one macros ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
--   op                   lowers to                waits on
--   draw                 move                     declared (#137)
--   discard              choose + move            declared (#137)
--   damage               move                     declared (#137)
--   mill                 move                     bind
--   addLife              move                     declared (#137)
--   lifeDownTo           move                     maths
--   energyMarker         modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   look                 reveal                   audience, branch
--   ko                   move                     declared (#274)
--   switchMode           modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   power                modifyAttr               declared (#273)
--   comboPower           modifyAttr               declared (#273)
--   grant                modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   negateSkills         negate                   declared (#276)
--   negateSkillsOfKind   negate                   declared (#276)
--   hidden               modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   redirectAttack       modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   comboFrom            move + negate            branch, cause
--   flip                 modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   faceUp               modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   addMarker            modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   removeMarker         modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   costReduction        costModifier             declared (#277)
--   negateKeyword        negate                   declared (#276)
--   gains                modifyAttr               declared (#275)
--   replaceLeave         replace                  declared (#137)
--   altCost              costModifier             declared (#277)
--   resolvingPlay        replace                  branch
--   negateAttack         replace                  declared (#137)
--   negateCounter        replace                  declared (#137)
--   negateOwnSkill       negate                   declared (#276)
--   may                  chooseMode               declared (#273)
--
-- `branch` is the one grammar question left: three of the five rows still
-- out wait on it (`look` also on `audience`), `lifeDownTo` on `maths`, and
-- `mill` on `bind`, which no grammar change here would answer.

-- ── the two numbers a card changes (§2.3, 20-5) ────────────────────────────
--
-- A change to power or combo power is one act — one attribute of one card,
-- by an expression, for a duration — and `modifyAttr` is that act. The two
-- rows differ in nothing but which attribute they name, which is the whole of
-- the argument the spec's table makes.

-- §20-5 — "+5000 power for the turn", "+X power", "power +5000 for each …"
DEFINE OP power
  TAKES (target: ref, amount: amount, until: duration)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: power, amount: $amount, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the card's power changes by the amount, for the duration"
  doc: "what `power` means: one attribute of one card, changed by an expression until a moment"

-- §20-5 — "+10000 combo power for the turn"
DEFINE OP comboPower
  TAKES (target: ref, amount: amount, until: duration)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: comboPower, amount: $amount, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the card's combo power changes by the amount, for the duration"
  doc: "the same act as `power`, on the other printed number"

-- ── the offer to decline (§2.3, 20-16) ──────────────────────────────────────
--
-- "You may draw 1 card" is a `chooseMode` with two options, the second empty —
-- the shape `chooseMode`'s own primitive case now reads (#273): it still asks
-- when one branch is empty rather than dropping it, asks `chooser` in place of
-- the master when the call names one, and binds `did(what: may)` from which
-- branch was picked either way, so "if you do" / "if you don't" reads it.

-- §20-16 — "You may draw 1 card", "your opponent may KO 1 of their cards"
DEFINE OP may
  TAKES (ops: ops, reason: string, chooser: side)
  DO {
    chooseMode(modes: ["Do it" $ops, "Don't" {}], reason: $reason, chooser: $chooser)
  }
  text: "the chooser may do the program, or not"
  doc: "what `may` means: `chooseMode` with the declined branch empty — the primitive is the general form, this is the printed shape"

-- ── a move told apart by its cause (§2.5-2, #274) ───────────────────────────
--
-- `damage`, `ko`, a plain `draw` and every other move that isn't just "an
-- effect moved a card" are one primitive — `moveTo` — with `cause` naming
-- which. `ko` came first (#274): its `target` is a `ref`. `draw`, `damage`
-- and `addLife` followed once a selector's count could be handed an `amount`
-- (#137, the `count` entry above): each is the top `n` cards of one pile,
-- moved, and `moveAs` (`engine/script-schema.ts`) reads each of the three
-- shapes back as its own spelling before either engine runs it — a draw from
-- an empty deck, damage's own moments (21-3) and "both players" (the top `n`
-- of *each* pile) are what those cases know and a plain move does not.
-- `discard` is 20-7 written out: the hand's owner chooses, and the cards go —
-- `discardAs` folds the two steps back into the one `discard` whose case asks
-- each player in turn. `mill` waits on `bind`, `lifeDownTo` on `maths`.

-- §20-5 — "draw 1 card", "your opponent draws 2 cards", "draw X cards",
-- "draw cards until you have 4 cards in your hand"
DEFINE OP draw
  TAKES (n: amount, side: side)
  DO {
    moveTo(target: TOP $n IN $side.deck, to: hand, cause: draw)
  }
  text: "the top cards of the player's deck move to their hand, as a draw"
  doc: "what `draw` means: the general move, from the top of the deck, with the cause a draw carries"

-- §20-7 — "discard 1 card from your hand", "your opponent chooses 1 card in
-- their hand and discards it", "…to the Warp"
DEFINE OP discard
  TAKES (n: amount, side: side, to: area)
  DO {
    choose(sel: $n IN $side.hand, as: "discarded", chooser: $side)
    moveTo(target: $discarded, to: $to, reveal: true)
  }
  text: "the hand's owner chooses that many of its cards, and they go to the Drop Area or the Warp"
  doc: "what `discard` means: a choice the hand's owner makes, then the move — the one row over two primitives"

-- §21-3 — "deal 1 damage to your opponent"
DEFINE OP damage
  TAKES (n: amount, side: side)
  DO {
    moveTo(target: TOP $n IN $side.life, to: hand, cause: damage)
  }
  text: "the top life cards of the player move to their hand, as damage"
  doc: "what `damage` means: the general move, from the life, with the cause damage carries — never Critical (21-3)"

-- §3-9 — "add the top card of your deck to your life"
DEFINE OP addLife
  TAKES (n: amount, side: side)
  DO {
    moveTo(target: TOP $n IN $side.deck, to: life)
  }
  text: "the top cards of the player's deck move to their life"
  doc: "what `addLife` means: the general move, from the top of the deck to the Life Area"

-- §1-13, §21-3-3 — "KO 1 of your opponent's Battle Cards." To the owner's
-- Drop with the cause "when you KO'd a card" (20-16's `did`) and "when this
-- card is KO'd" (9-6) both read. [Indestructible], "can't be KO'd by
-- skills" and a KO's own replacement offers are the general move's business
-- (9-10) — the macro says only the destination and the cause.
DEFINE OP ko
  TAKES (target: ref)
  DO {
    moveTo(target: $target, to: drop, cause: ko)
  }
  text: "the card moves to its owner's Drop Area"
  doc: "what `ko` means: the general move, to the Drop, with the cause every KO carries"

-- ── a rule that stops applying (§2.2, 9-1-5, #276) ──────────────────────────
--
-- Four spellings, one primitive: `negate(target, what: skills | kind | keyword
-- | own, kind?, keyword?, until?)` reads a card's skills, a printed kind of
-- them, a named keyword everywhere, or the skill resolving now, and stops it
-- for a duration. `negateAs` (`engine/script-schema.ts`) is the interpreter's
-- own reading in the other direction — a `negate(…)` call read back as one of
-- the four — and each row here is built to invert it exactly, so a program
-- written the declared way and one written the primitive's own way say the
-- same thing (`verify/vm.ts`'s existing macro/primitive parity block).
-- `comboFrom`'s negate half stays out: it needs `cause` too (#274), not
-- declared on this branch.

-- §9-1-5 — "negate that card's skills for the turn"
DEFINE OP negateSkills
  TAKES (target: ref, until: duration)
  DO {
    negate(target: $target, what: skills, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the target's skills stop applying, for the duration"
  doc: "what `negateSkills` means: every skill of one card, negated (9-1-5)"

-- §9-1-5 — "negate that card's [Auto] skill for the turn"
DEFINE OP negateSkillsOfKind
  TAKES (target: ref, kind: word, until: duration)
  DO {
    negate(target: $target, what: kind, kind: $kind, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the target's skills of the printed kind stop applying, for the duration"
  doc: "what `negateSkillsOfKind` means: one printed kind of skill of one card, negated — not the whole card (9-1-5)"

-- §9-1-5 — "negate this card's [Energy-Exhaust] skill in all areas"
DEFINE OP negateKeyword
  TAKES (keyword: word, target: ref)
  DO {
    negate(target: $target, what: keyword, keyword: $keyword)
  }
  text: "the target loses the named keyword skill"
  doc: "what `negateKeyword` means: one named keyword taken away, wherever the card sits — no duration of its own, unlike the other three"

-- §9-1-5 — "negate this skill for the turn / battle / game"
DEFINE OP negateOwnSkill
  TAKES (until: duration)
  DO {
    negate(what: own, until: $until)
  }
  text: "this skill does not happen again, for the duration"
  doc: "what `negateOwnSkill` means: the skill resolving now, negated for a span read off its own card — never a target, the way the other three take one"

-- ── a price is not a number (§2.5-4, #277) ──────────────────────────────────
--
-- `costModifier(target, amount?, what?, colors?, skillKind?, pay?, n?, for?,
-- alt?, orbs?, until?)` is the one primitive under both spellings —
-- `costModifierAs` (`engine/script-schema.ts`) reads a call back as whichever
-- it stands for, and each row here is built to invert it exactly: `pay`
-- present means `altCost` (the price replaced), absent means `costReduction`
-- (the price's number changed). The rules engine reads only the reduction
-- half, through the `costOf`/`specifiedCost` layers `LAYER_KINDS` pairs it
-- with (`vm/effects.ts`); `altCost` stays a documented gap there (#149).

-- §20-21 — "reduce the energy cost of your <Son Goku> cards in your hand by
-- 1", "…combo cost…", "…Z-Energy cost…", the specified (coloured) half
-- (owner's BT19-039 ruling), and a skill's own orb price scoped by kind.
DEFINE OP costReduction
  TAKES (target: ref, amount: amount, what: word, skillKind: word, colors: colors, until: duration)
  DO {
    costModifier(target: $target, amount: $amount, what: $what, skillKind: $skillKind, colors: $colors, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the target's price changes by the amount"
  doc: "what `costReduction` means: the general price change, with nothing about what replaces it"

-- §5-3 — "you can activate this card's [Counter] skill for nothing / by
-- adding cards from your life to your hand / by a reduced energy price /
-- by a program instead"
DEFINE OP altCost
  TAKES (pay: word, n: number, for: word, ops: ops, orbs: colors, target: ref, until: duration)
  DO {
    costModifier(target: $target, pay: $pay, n: $n, for: $for, alt: $ops, orbs: $orbs, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the target's price is replaced"
  doc: "what `altCost` means: the general price change, naming what replaces the price rather than a number it moves by"

-- ── a player and the battle in progress, as subjects (§2.5-1, §2.5-3, #275) ─
--
-- `modifyAttr` reached one card and six attributes before this issue; the
-- nine rows below are what unblocked once it also reached a *player*
-- (`side`, for `energyMarkers`) and the *battle in progress* (`target` read
-- as the value being set rather than a locator — there is only one), and
-- once `mode`, `markers`, `keywords`, `hidden`, `faceUp` and `flipped` were
-- declared card attributes (`attributes.rules`). `modifyAttrAs`
-- (`engine/script-schema.ts`) is the one reading of each spelling, so a
-- program written either way runs the same case, puts the same effect in
-- force (or none) and reads in the same words — the precedent `negateAs`
-- and `costModifierAs` set for their own primitives.

-- §1-14 — "gain 1 energy marker", "your opponent gains 2 energy markers"
DEFINE OP energyMarker
  TAKES (n: amount, side: side)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(subject: player, side: $side, attr: energyMarkers, amount: $n)
  }
  text: "the named player's energy markers change by the amount"
  doc: "what `energyMarker` means: `modifyAttr`'s first widened subject — a player rather than a card (spec §2.5-1)"

-- §8-1, §22-4-2 — "switch the target of the attack to it"
DEFINE OP redirectAttack
  TAKES (target: ref)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(subject: battle, attr: guard, target: $target)
  }
  text: "the target of the attack switches to the named card"
  doc: "what `redirectAttack` means: `modifyAttr`'s other widened subject — the battle in progress, which has one attribute and needs no locator of its own (spec §2.5-1)"

-- §1-10 — "switch it to Rest Mode", "switch it to Active Mode"
DEFINE OP switchMode
  TAKES (target: ref, mode: word)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: mode, mode: $mode)
  }
  text: "the card switches to the named mode"
  doc: "what `switchMode` means: one of the six card attributes spec §2.5-3 adds — set to the named mode, no duration of its own (1-10)"

-- §23-5 — "switch it to Hidden Mode", "switch it to Revealed Mode"
DEFINE OP hidden
  TAKES (target: ref, hidden: boolean)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: hidden, flag: $hidden)
  }
  text: "the card switches to Hidden Mode or Revealed Mode, as named"
  doc: "what `hidden` means: set to the named boolean, no duration of its own (23-5)"

-- §3-9-2-1 — "flip up to 1 card in your life face up", "…face down"
DEFINE OP faceUp
  TAKES (target: ref, faceUp: boolean)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: faceUp, flag: $faceUp)
  }
  text: "the card in life turns face up or face down, as named"
  doc: "what `faceUp` means: set to the named boolean, no duration of its own (3-9-2-1)"

-- §22-2-4 — "you may flip this card over"
DEFINE OP flip
  TAKES (target: ref)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: flipped)
  }
  text: "the card turns to its back side"
  doc: "what `flip` means: the one card attribute with no value field of its own to name — the primitive's own case reads what a flip means (22-2-4)"

-- §1-11 — "add 1 marker to this card"
DEFINE OP addMarker
  TAKES (target: ref, n: amount)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: markers, amount: $n, sign: add)
  }
  text: "the card's markers increase by the amount"
  doc: "what `addMarker` means: a delta on the one attribute a marker count is (1-11); `sign` is which way `removeMarker` beside it moves the same delta"

-- §1-11 — "remove 1 marker from this card"
DEFINE OP removeMarker
  TAKES (target: ref, n: amount)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: markers, amount: $n, sign: remove)
  }
  text: "the card's markers decrease by the amount"
  doc: "the same act as `addMarker`, the other way (1-11)"

-- §9-1-3, §22-1 — "this card gains [Blocker] for the turn"
DEFINE OP grant
  TAKES (target: ref, keyword: keyword, until: duration)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: keywords, keyword: $keyword, until: $until)
  }
  text: "the target gains the named keyword skill, for the duration"
  doc: "what `grant` means: one card attribute, by the keyword it counts as having — `until` left out means the keyword is printed rather than granted, which nothing calls this row for (9-1-3, 22-1)"

-- §20-1 — "this card also counts as ≪Saiyan≫", "…is also treated as
-- red", "…is also treated as {Planet Namek}"
DEFINE OP gains
  TAKES (target: ref, traits: strings, characters: strings, colors: strings, names: strings)
  DO {
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: traits, values: $traits)
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: characters, values: $characters)
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: colors, values: $colors)
    modifyAttr(target: $target, attr: alsoNames, values: $names)
  }
  text: "the card also counts as the named traits, characters, colours and names, wherever it is"
  doc: "what `gains` means: `modifyAttr`'s general row, one call per list attribute — a call that leaves one out leaves that call's own \"values\" out too, which the static reader (`collectStatics`/`permanents`) already treats as none of that list (docs/arena-ruleset-spec.md §2.3)"

-- ── an event in another's place (§2.2, 9-10, #137) ──────────────────────────
--
-- `replace(event, with, by?, bySide?, to?, optional?, target?)` is the one
-- primitive under three spellings here. `replaceLeave` is its redirect form:
-- the departure happens, to somewhere else, and `with` is one move of the
-- card itself — the shape `collectStatics` and `vm/effects.ts` already read
-- as a redirect, so a [Permanent] written either way stands the same offer
-- under the same kind. `by: ko` on a `leave` is the same moment as the `ko`
-- event (the readers already store both as `by: "ko"`); it is spelled that
-- way because a template cannot pick its `event` by the call's `by`. A
-- `replaceLeave` to `play` or `under` — which the compiler never writes, and
-- the sweep proves no record carries — would lower to a substitute rather
-- than the native op's mapped redirect, the one boundary of this row.
--
-- `negateAttack` and `negateCounter` are the attack in progress and the
-- [Counter] being answered, each replaced by nothing. Neither is a standing
-- offer: `replaceAs` reads the call back as the spelling it stands for, so
-- their own cases — 20-16's "if you negated a Leader Card's attack", 9-7's
-- counter in flight — stay the one reading of either.
-- `resolvingPlay` waits on `branch` (the header).

-- §9-10 — "if this card would be KO'd, place it in your Warp instead", "…
-- would be removed from the Battle Area by your opponent's skill, you may
-- return it to your hand instead"
DEFINE OP replaceLeave
  TAKES (to: area, by: word, bySide: word, mode: word, optional: boolean, target: ref)
  DO {
    replace(event: leave, by: $by, bySide: $bySide, optional: $optional, target: $target, with: { moveTo(target: [subject], to: $to, mode: $mode) })
  }
  text: "if the card would leave the Battle Area, it goes to the named area instead"
  doc: "what `replaceLeave` means: `replace`'s redirect form — the departure happens, to another area"

-- §8-1-6-1 — "negate the attack"
DEFINE OP negateAttack
  TAKES ()
  DO {
    replace(event: attack, with: {})
  }
  text: "the attack in progress resolves to nothing"
  doc: "what `negateAttack` means: the attack, replaced by nothing"

-- §9-7 — "negate the [Counter] skill"
DEFINE OP negateCounter
  TAKES ()
  DO {
    replace(event: counter, with: {})
  }
  text: "the [Counter] this one answers resolves to nothing"
  doc: "what `negateCounter` means: the counter being answered, replaced by nothing (9-7)"
